the latin letter z doesn't even appear  in russia's cyrillic alphabet but since  the start of the war in ukraine it's  gone from being a mysterious marking on  russian military vehicles to becoming a  recognizable pro-war symbol used to  express support of president putin and  the russian military  what's more the russian government has  begun merging this new symbol with the  orange and black stripes of a  centuries-old ribbon a ribbon that has  been loaded with military meaning  throughout russian history so where are  these symbols coming from and how is the  kremlin using them to drum up support  for the war  let's start with the z if you look at  russian signage or social media you'll  see it all over the place these days for  example this z post from the russian  military's instagram account appears  alongside the russian word for heroes  with a z added to the end other photos  appear to show russian soldiers flashing  the z with their hands when president  putin spoke at a rally in march zee was  featured in signs around him that read  for russia and for a world without  nazism we'll come back to that nazism  theme in a bit there are a lot of  theories about what the z means and  where it came from but my colleague  matthew luxmore who studied the use of  russian symbols says it's very likely  that it was just an easy to write  marking like a v or an o russia used it  to identify some of its military  equipment and then it spread it does  seem like it was kind of bottom up and  people started to write z's on their  cars because they wanted to express  their support for this campaign and then  very quickly it was seized on by the  government now that z has become a thing  you may notice it written with stripes  like this and that is not some organic  thing matthew says that is very  intentional this really really works it  resonates people people understand it  people have pride in this why are those  stripes significant you have to go back  a few centuries to find out  it turns out those stripes go back to  the 1700s at a time when russia was  expanding its empire and seizing parts  of what's now modern-day ukraine in 1769  catherine the great established russia's  highest military honor the order of  saint george  the really interesting thing about the  order in addition to its exclusivity is  the ribbon it's actually a point of some  contention among scholars where the  ribbon comes from but it's also the most  recognized ribbon and today  used for lots of different co-opted for  lots of different reasons history  professor russell martin says that over  time russians have used the ribbon of  saint george to rally around various  military achievements most prominently  it's become a symbol of the soviet  victory over the nazis in world war ii  something russia celebrates with a huge  victory day parade every may  in recent years the ribbon has also been  used to express support for the 2014  annexation of crimea from ukraine and  russia's military intervention in the  donbas region for some it's going to  have a  general patriotic  purpose but for  for most and since 2014 especially it  really does have to do with russia's  military greatness it has  association  in the with in the past with expansion  and that's where the ribbon of saint  george and the z come together over the  years president putin and his supporters  have seized on this idea that russia  continues to fight nazism fascism and  threats from the west and now putin is  using this same idea to justify the  bloody invasion of ukraine that's  playing out today  they're being ruled  as putin puts it by nazis neo-nazis and  fascists we need to save them from the  neo-nazis they they just don't realize  it themselves there's no evidence that  ukraine's government is run by neo-nazis  and ukraine's president volodomir  zielinski is jewish but as these russian  social media posts show the kremlin is  clearly merging the new symbol z with  the powerful symbolism of the ribbon of  saint george to try to tap into this  world war ii narrative that russia must  continue to defeat nazism that russia is  for victory those two things are being  put together to kind of conflate both  support for the armed forces and this  deep-rooted idea that somehow russia is  um  waging the same campaign and waged them  defending itself rather than invading  another country  yeah it's always said it's defending  itself as um hard that is to i guess  believe based on the evidence of course  not everyone buys this argument and some  have likened russia's z to the nazi  swastika for example ukraine's defense  minister posted this photo on twitter  showing a swastika-like symbol formed by  two z's and other comparisons of the z  and the swastika have been photographed  around the world in recent weeks now  that the z has been embraced by the  russian government ukraine's foreign  minister is calling on all states to  criminalize the use of z as a way to  publicly support the war  speaking of symbols the javelin has  become a symbol of the ukrainian  resistance check out this video to learn  more about what it is and how it's being  used on the battlefield and if you like  this video subscribe to the channel for  more stories like this i'll see you next  time
 

